{"agent":"fx-triage-guard","data":{"by":"deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4-Pro-0813-Godzila","evidence":{"chunk_findings":[{"chunk":2,"detail":"`path_snapshot` drops every path whose entry is absent from `GE.worktree_entries`. If a post-round change is a deletion, the deleted path is absent from the worktree and, when it was not in `recorded`, it is also absent from `now`. `snapshot_differing` then takes `recorded.keys() | now.keys()`, which never contains that deleted path, so no difference is reported. Concrete input: `recorded={\"a.py\": \"100644 abc\"}` and `now_changed=[\"b.py\"]`, where `b.py` was tracked but deleted after the review. Assuming `a.py` is unchanged, `snapshot_differing` returns `[]`; the guard lets an unreviewed deletion pass, violating the stated fail-closed behavior and the requirement that any edit to a changed/reviewed file after the round must block.","digest":"b1c43c1caa0d7562","location":"ddflow/services/gates/reviewed_paths.py:24","severity":"HIGH","title":"ddflow/services/gates/reviewed_paths.py:24"},{"chunk":3,"detail":"The fallback test only asserts that the final gate outcome is `\"failed\"`. It creates two untracked files: `repo/other.txt` in the primary repo and `wt/notes.md` in the item worktree. If the whole-tree fallback were buggy and scanned the primary repo instead of the item worktree, it would still see `repo/other.txt` and return `\"failed\"`, so this assertion would pass. The test therefore does not verify that the fallback compares the correct tree; it should assert that the failure names `notes.md` and/or excludes `other.txt`.","digest":"f49fa45c69acc742","location":"tests/test_review_triage_scoped_tree.py:104","severity":"LOW","title":"tests/test_review_triage_scoped_tree.py:104"}],"chunks_total":3,"coverage":"3/3 chunk(s) reviewed","delta_rounds":0,"diff_chars":10533,"diff_sha":"4ae93ba7c7ead87a2e516ec0b2d4945ccb2336ad00c7f749e4ec394db6cf7203","diff_source":"main..HEAD + tracked working-tree changes in [REDACTED:path]","diff_st